"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,040\nThe Learning Brain.\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,513\nEpisode four.\n\n3\n00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:09,669\nThe power of prediction.\n\n4\n00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:15,476\nWatch a ball roll toward the edge of a table.\n\n5\n00:00:15,705 --> 00:00:16,831\nBefore it falls,\n\n6\n00:00:16,851 --> 00:00:19,179\nyou've already pictured where it will land.\n\n7\n00:00:19,429 --> 00:00:21,907\nYour brain isn't just taking in the world.\n\n8\n00:00:22,104 --> 00:00:24,667\nIt's constantly guessing what comes next.\n\n9\n00:00:24,737 --> 00:00:28,239\nOne influential theory says perception works this way.\n\n10\n00:00:28,530 --> 00:00:31,053\nHigher areas send predictions down.\n\n11\n00:00:31,382 --> 00:00:32,456\nOnly the mismatch,\n\n12\n00:00:32,476 --> 00:00:34,520\nthe part the guess got wrong,\n\n13\n00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:35,694\ntravels back up.\n\n14\n00:00:36,071 --> 00:00:38,637\nThat mismatch is called prediction error.\n\n15\n00:00:38,708 --> 00:00:41,147\nThe clearest evidence comes from reward.\n\n16\n00:00:41,453 --> 00:00:42,395\nIn monkeys,\n\n17\n00:00:42,415 --> 00:00:46,259\ndopamine cells fire when juice arrives unexpectedly.\n\n18\n00:00:46,617 --> 00:00:49,404\nAfter the monkey learns that a light comes first,\n\n19\n00:00:49,506 --> 00:00:51,662\nthe cells fire at the light instead,\n\n20\n00:00:51,682 --> 00:00:53,861\nand barely respond to the juice.\n\n21\n00:00:54,151 --> 00:00:56,975\nAnd if the juice is expected but never comes,\n\n22\n00:00:57,032 --> 00:00:58,087\nthey go quiet,\n\n23\n00:00:58,266 --> 00:00:59,648\nright when it was due.\n\n24\n00:00:59,718 --> 00:01:01,943\nThat error signal drives learning.\n\n25\n00:01:02,223 --> 00:01:04,485\nIn a classic model of conditioning,\n\n26\n00:01:04,582 --> 00:01:09,267\nhow much you learn on each trial depends on how wrong your prediction was.\n\n27\n00:01:09,539 --> 00:01:11,600\nA big error makes a big update.\n\n28\n00:01:11,995 --> 00:01:14,253\nOnce the outcome is fully predicted,\n\n29\n00:01:14,343 --> 00:01:16,015\nthere's nothing left to learn.\n\n30\n00:01:16,085 --> 00:01:18,604\nPriming was the quiet version of this.\n\n31\n00:01:18,838 --> 00:01:21,055\nA warmed-up network is a prediction,\n\n32\n00:01:21,155 --> 00:01:23,412\nand surprise is what changes it.\n\n33\n00:01:23,482 --> 00:01:25,694\nIt shows up in lecture halls too.\n\n34\n00:01:26,038 --> 00:01:27,309\nIn a physics course,\n\n35\n00:01:27,413 --> 00:01:33,133\nstudents who just watched demonstrations understood them no better than students who never saw them.\n\n36\n00:01:33,515 --> 00:01:36,527\nStudents who first predicted the outcome did better.\n\n37\n00:01:36,597 --> 00:01:37,717\nOne caution.\n\n38\n00:01:38,041 --> 00:01:40,065\nThe reward evidence is strong,\n\n39\n00:01:40,153 --> 00:01:44,652\nbut the idea that the whole brain runs on prediction is still a theory,\n\n40\n00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:46,596\nand an actively debated one.\n\n41\n00:01:46,971 --> 00:01:49,245\nAnd the classroom gains were real,\n\n42\n00:01:49,265 --> 00:01:50,259\nbut modest.\n\n43\n00:01:50,329 --> 00:01:52,333\nSo before you reveal a result,\n\n44\n00:01:52,353 --> 00:01:53,674\nask for a guess.\n\n45\n00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:55,492\nBefore a demonstration,\n\n46\n00:01:55,512 --> 00:01:56,128\na reading,\n\n47\n00:01:56,148 --> 00:01:57,635\nor a worked problem,\n\n48\n00:01:57,655 --> 00:02:00,322\nhave students commit to a prediction in writing.\n\n49\n00:02:00,611 --> 00:02:02,609\nA wrong guess is not wasted.\n\n50\n00:02:02,904 --> 00:02:05,815\nIt's the signal that tells the brain to update.\n\n51\n00:02:05,885 --> 00:02:09,820\nThe brain learns from the gap between what it expected and what it got.\n\n52\n00:02:10,158 --> 00:02:11,682\nMake room for that gap.\n\n53\n00:02:11,859 --> 00:02:13,690\nThat's the power of prediction.\n"
